# Trivy suppressions for eks-secure-baseline — TERRAFORM (infra/) ONLY.
#
# POLICY
# ------
# This file is passed explicitly with --ignorefile when scanning infra/.
# The Kubernetes scans use their own ignore files so that a suppression can
# never leak across scan targets:
#
#   infra/              -> .trivyignore                (this file)
#   k8s/hardening/      -> NO ignore file — fully gated, no exceptions
#   k8s/compliance/     -> .trivyignore-kube-bench     (scanner Job only)
#
# The scanner is NEVER weakened: every scan runs
# --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --exit-code 1. Do not add a blanket severity
# filter, do not remove --exit-code, and do not add an entry here without a
# written justification.
#
# Two classes of exception are recorded below and they are NOT equivalent:
#
#   NOT REMEDIABLE   — the control cannot be satisfied by any configuration
#                      that preserves the component's function (AWS-0104).
#   ACCEPTED RISK    — the control IS remediable, but only by a design the
#                      project has explicitly rejected, and a compensating
#                      control is in place (AWS-0040, AWS-0041).
#
# Reviewed: 2026-09-19

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS-0104 — "Security group rule allows egress to multiple public internet
#             addresses" (aws_vpc_security_group_egress_rule.cluster_egress)
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT REMEDIABLE for an EKS control plane security group.
#
# The EKS control plane's cross-account ENIs must reach worker node kubelets
# on ephemeral ports and regional AWS service endpoints (STS, ECR, CloudWatch
# Logs, EC2). Those endpoints are not expressible as a stable set of CIDRs or
# prefix lists for this security group, and AWS's own EKS security group
# requirements specify outbound 0.0.0.0/0 for the cluster security group.
# Narrowing it breaks node registration and add-on installation — failures
# that surface only after a full ~15 minute apply.
#
# Compensating controls:
#   * Attached ONLY to AWS-managed control plane ENIs; no workload traffic.
#   * The SG has NO ingress rules at all: it is egress-only.
#   * Workload egress is separately default-denied by the NetworkPolicies in
#     k8s/hardening/network-policies.yaml, which also block the instance
#     metadata endpoint (169.254.169.254).
#   * Nodes have no public IPs and sit in private subnets behind a NAT gateway.
AWS-0104

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS-0040 — "Public cluster access is enabled"
#             (aws_eks_cluster.main: endpoint_public_access = true)
# AWS-0041 — "Cluster allows access from a public CIDR"
#             (aws_eks_cluster.main: public_access_cidrs)
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ACCEPTED RISK — remediable in principle, deliberately not remediated.
#
# These two checks are only satisfiable by endpoint_public_access = false
# (a fully private API endpoint). AWS-0041 in particular cannot be satisfied
# by ANY real administrative address: it requires a private CIDR range, so
# even a single-host /32 of a genuine public IP still fails it. Locking the
# allowlist — which this project does — does not and cannot clear it.
#
# A fully private endpoint was evaluated and rejected for this build: CI runs
# on GitHub-hosted runners, so a private endpoint requires a self-hosted
# runner, a bastion, or a VPN inside the VPC. That is a Tier-2
# re-architecture with new always-on infrastructure and cost, not a
# configuration fix, and it was out of scope for an internal hardening
# baseline.
#
# WHAT IS ACTUALLY IN PLACE (the compensating control, and it is real):
#   * public_access_cidrs is locked to a SINGLE administrative host (/32).
#     It is NOT 0.0.0.0/0. The network path is open to exactly one address.
#   * infra/variables.tf enforces this in code: validation blocks REJECT
#     0.0.0.0/0 and reject any prefix broader than /24. The open-endpoint
#     configuration that these checks warn about cannot be reintroduced by
#     editing a variable — terraform refuses to plan.
#   * scripts/verify-api-restriction.sh asserts the live cluster's
#     resolved public_access_cidrs on EVERY deploy, so drift between the
#     declared and effective allowlist fails the pipeline.
#   * CI's temporary access is self-scoped and self-revoking: a single /32
#     for the runner, added and removed by scripts/ci-api-access.sh via a
#     'trap ... EXIT INT TERM' so the entry is withdrawn even on failure,
#     abort, cancellation or timeout. Steady state is always the single
#     administrative /32.
#   * endpoint_private_access is also enabled, so in-VPC traffic never
#     traverses the public endpoint.
#   * Network reachability is the OUTERMOST layer only: AWS IAM
#     authentication and Kubernetes RBAC both still apply, and the audit
#     log records every API call.
#
# NOT HIDDEN FROM THE REPORT: this suppression affects the CI gate only.
# kube-bench still assesses the API endpoint against the CIS EKS Benchmark
# on every run, and that assessment appears in the published compliance
# report. The exception is recorded here so the gate reflects a reviewed
# decision, not so the finding disappears.
#
# REVISIT IF: a compliance requirement forbids any public endpoint, or a
# self-hosted runner / bastion becomes available — at which point
# endpoint_public_access = false clears both checks for real and these two
# entries should be deleted.
AWS-0040
AWS-0041
